      "question": "I want to know if Transformer 1 at MAIN is running hot compared with its load. Over the available data window, summarize top-oil temperature (oti), its alarm and trip readings (oti_a, oti_t), and the three phase currents (il1, il2, il3) with neutral current (inut), giving min, max and mean for each. Then tell me whether the highest oti reading happened at the same timestamp as the highest total phase current. Flag any phase whose mean current differs from the other phases' average by more than 10%. If oti has no data, use ati for context and say that the thermal correlation can't be confirmed.",
      "answer": "**Transformer 1 (MAIN)**\n\nOver 5 daily readings from 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19, top-oil temperature changes only slightly with total current. The alarm and trip readings point to a thermal condition, but this data is too thin to link it to load. There is oti data, so the ati fallback wasn't needed.\n\n**Summary**\n\n| Sensor | Min | Max | Mean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| oti (\u00b0C) | 84 | 87 | 85.4 |\n| oti_a | 1 | 2 | 1.8 |\n| oti_t | 1 | 1 | 1.0 |\n| il1 (A) | 100 | 102 | 100.6 |\n| il2 (A) | 103 | 106 | 104.6 |\n| il3 (A) | 94 | 96 | 95.0 |\n| inut (A) | 5 | 6 | 5.2 |\n\n**Does the hottest reading match the highest load?** Partly.\n- The highest oti (87 \u00b0C) was on **2024-01-16 00:00**. Total phase current that day was 301 A, the highest in the window.\n- But 2024-01-18 also had 301 A, and oti was only 86 \u00b0C.\n- Across the window, total current only ranged from 299 to 301 A while oti ranged from 84 to 87 \u00b0C. Oil temperature roughly follows load, but a 2 A range over 5 points is too small to show a real link.\n\n**Phase imbalance (more than 10% from the other two phases' average):** No phase is flagged.\n- il1: 100.6 A vs 99.8 A, **+0.8%**\n- il2: 104.6 A vs 97.8 A, **+7.0%**. This is the highest but under the 10% limit.\n- il3: 95.0 A vs 102.6 A, **\u22127.4%**. This is the lowest, also under the limit.\n\n**Is it running hot for its load?**\n- **The trip reading (oti_t) is 1 in every sample, and the alarm reading (oti_a) is 1 or 2.** If 1 means the flag is active, this is worth checking whatever the load. I'm not sure how to read an alarm value of 2.\n- Oil is at 84\u201387 \u00b0C, only about 7 \u00b0C above ambient (ati 77\u201379 \u00b0C, mean 78 \u00b0C). That suggests the heat is mostly coming from the hot surroundings, not from load.\n- Current stays nearly constant, so load doesn't explain the heat or the alarm readings.\n- The sensor data looks unreliable. The winding temperature (wti, 79\u201382 \u00b0C) is *lower* than the oil temperature, which normally shouldn't happen. The currents (about 100 A) and phase-to-neutral voltages (about 110 V) are also very small for a transformer rated at 100 MVA. I'd check sensor calibration and units before trusting any load-versus-heat conclusion.",
      "score": {
